{"name":"Blue Sarong","hex":"#9ad6e8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"sarong","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A garment made of a length of printed cloth wrapped about the waist that is commonly worn by men and women in South and Southeast Asia, also in the Pacific islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sarong"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sarong","blocks":[{"text":"A sarong or a sarung is a large tube or length of fabric, often wrapped around the waist, worn in Southeast Asia, South Asia, West Asia, North Africa, East Africa, West Africa, and on many Pacific islands. The fabric may be brightly colored by means of batik or ikat dyeing. Many modern sarongs have printed designs, often depicting animals or plants. Different types of sarongs are worn in different places in the world, notably the lungi in the Indian subcontinent and the izaar in the Arabian Peninsula."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Nederlandse_militair_in_sarong_TMnr_10029658.jpg/330px-COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Nederlandse_militair_in_sarong_TMnr_10029658.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarong","wikidata":"Q322781"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Jungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_%281905%29_%2814593378878%29.jpg/500px-Jungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_%281905%29_%2814593378878%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Jungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_%281905%29_%2814593378878%29.jpg/1280px-Jungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_%281905%29_%2814593378878%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Jungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_%281905%29_%2814593378878%29.jpg/500px-Jungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_%281905%29_%2814593378878%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Three women wearing sarongs in 1905","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJungle_trails_and_jungle_people_-_travel%2C_adventure_and_abservation_in_the_Far_East_(1905)_(145933788